<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Tamanian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Tamanian was an Armenian architect trained in St Petersburg, where he worked before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>. Moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> in 1923, he drew up the master plan of the modern city, with its circular and radial layout, and designed landmarks such as the Opera and Ballet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> and Government House on Republic Square. He is regarded as the father of modern Yerevan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-tamanian.jpg" alt="Alexander Tamanian" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Tamanyan_(1903).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander Hovhannesi Tamanian was born on 4 March 1878 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Yekaterinodar</a>, in a banker's family, and graduated from the St Petersburg Academy of Arts in 1904. His early works were neoclassical, among them the mansion of V. P. Kochubei at Tsarskoye Selo in 1911 to 1912, the house of Prince S. A. Scherbatov on Novinsky Boulevard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1911 to 1913, railway employees' housing and a tuberculosis sanatorium near Moscow, and the central workshops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> railway at Lyubertsy in 1916. He became an Academician of Architecture in 1914 and in 1917 was elected Vice-President of the Academy of Arts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1923 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> to head the new construction effort in the republic. He was chief engineer of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> and a member of the CEC of the Armenian SSR from 1925 to 1936. He designed layouts for Leninakan, now Gyumri, in 1925, Stepanakert in 1926, Nor-Bayazet, now Gavar, and Ahta-ahpara in 1927, and Echmiadzin in 1927 to 1928. His first general plan of modern Yerevan was approved in 1924, and his style helped turn a small provincial city into the capital and a major industrial and cultural centre.</p><p>He worked mainly in a neoclassical style with a national flavour, such as red tuff linings and traditional carving in stone. His best-known buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> include the first hydroelectric station of 1926, the Opera and Ballet theatre, built from 1926 to 1953, and Republic Square, built from 1926 to 1941. He chaired the Committee for the Protection of Historic Monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and was prominent in restoring historical landmarks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tamanian married Camilla Edwards, a maternal granddaughter of the architect Nicholas Benois. Their sons Gevorg and Yulius also became noted architects and continued his work. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> on 20 February 1936 and is buried at the Komitas Pantheon in the city centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Tamanian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alexander Tamanian</b> began his city plan for Yerevan, an institute for culture and history opened at Ejmiatsin in 1921, the Yerevan Opera Theatre and a dramatic theatre were built, and Martiros Saryan, Yeghishe Charents, Avetik Isahakyan and others produced popular works of art and literature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Krasnodar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.03,38.98&amp;year=1915" aria-label="Krasnodar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alexander Tamanian"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5541 6229h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5541 6229h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.03,38.98&amp;year=1915">What was here in 1915? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
